{% set local_problem %}
<p>Scottsboro sits in a debris zone. Pines along the lake drop needles 10 months of the year. Hardwoods dump leaves every fall. The humidity means that debris doesn't just sit in your gutters — it absorbs moisture, compacts, and turns into a dense sludge that regular cleaning can't handle.</p>

<p>We cleaned a house in <a href="/recent-projects/">Goose Pond Colony</a> last winter where the homeowner had skipped two years of maintenance. The debris in the gutters wasn't leaves anymore — it was a 3-inch layer of compressed organic material that had basically turned to soil. Growing things. Weeds actually sprouting in the gutters. That took 4 hours to clean instead of the usual 90 minutes. The quote tripled.</p>

<p>Here's the thing about debris in humid environments: it doesn't stay debris. It breaks down, holds water, creates weight. A foot of dry leaves weighs almost nothing. That same debris after a Scottsboro summer weighs 5-8 pounds per linear foot. On 150 feet of gutters, that's 750-1,200 pounds of weight on hangers designed to hold maybe 500 pounds total. Gutters sag. Hangers pull out. Fascia takes damage.</p>

<p>Regular cleaning prevents all of that. The question is whether "cleaning" means actual cleaning or a guy with a leaf blower spending 20 minutes on your roof.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Here's what we actually do, step by step:</p>

<p><strong>Debris removal by hand.</strong> We scoop debris out of the gutters into buckets. Not blow it onto your roof to wash back in. Not scatter it across your lawn. Remove it and dispose of it. This takes 45-60 minutes for most homes.</p>

<p><strong>Downspout flush.</strong> Every downspout gets water run through it until it flows clear at the bottom. If it's clogged, we clear it — snake, pressure, whatever it takes. This is the step most "cleaners" skip, and it's where 40% of drainage problems actually live. Another 15-20 minutes.</p>

<p><strong>System inspection.</strong> While we're up there, we check: gutter pitch (should be 1/4" drop per 10 feet toward the downspout), hanger condition (spacing should be 24" max, 18" in areas with ice potential), seam integrity, fascia condition behind the gutters. If something's wrong, we tell you. Another 15-20 minutes for thorough inspection.</p>

<p><strong>Ground cleanup.</strong> Whatever debris we dropped getting it out of the gutters, we clean up. Your property looks the same as when we arrived, minus the gutter problems. 10-15 minutes.</p>

<p>Total time: 90 minutes to 2 hours for a typical Scottsboro home. Anyone quoting 30-45 minutes is cutting corners. Usually the downspout flush and the inspection.</p>

<p>For vacation homes, we add photo documentation — before shots showing what we found, during shots showing the work, after shots showing the result. Sent to your email or phone same day.</p>
{% endset %}

